  For Expert players that's obvious.  MAG does not seem to be aimed at
expert players.  (Which is good.  Expert players won't take advice from
anyone.  Just look at this thread.  OTOH, mediocre players won't take
advice from anyone either.  The difference is that expert players
survive.)

  The problem with this thread is that, depending on the target
audience, any of the advice could be correct.  However, if limit
ourselves to players needing advice, then wishing seems suboptimal.


  Players that need advice on what to wish for are very unlikely to pick
the correct wish for their current (and near future) situation.  

  Successful wishes are even less likely, due to:
  - Wishing for artifacts when too many artifacts already exist.
  - Not ensuring the lamp is blessed.  
    - Most players that MAG is aimed at will be immediately #rubbing 
      any lamp they find.
  - Not ensuring luck is high enough.
    - I know this from bitter personal experience.
  - Typos
    - Copy/paste from the wishing spoiler is what got me through that 
      problem.
  - Not saving a magic lamp when they get multiple magic lamps.
    - Someone posted complaining that they got three magic lamps, and
      didn't get a wish from any of them.  They #rubbed them as they got
      them.  (Talk about kicking your luck in the teeth.)


  Let me repeat: Expert players can deal with #rubbing a magic lamp much
better.  They know what to wish for given their situation, how to make
the wish successful (blessing, luck, spelling, etc.), and how to make
the best use of the result.  I acknowledge that fully.  (Of course,
players at that level can just #dip fountains for wishes safely.)

  Fully explaining every possible aspect of wishing is similarly beyond
the scope of MAG.  (Such as, don't wish for a BoH or amulet of
reflection just before entering the top level of Sokoban for the first
time.)

  I also state that Expert players are not the intended audience for
MAG.  (Not that they'd take the advice anyway.)

  A permanent light source, OTOH, would be perfect for MAG's intended
audience.  Instead of having to assess, think, plan, and utilize in a
way they're not used to, they'd instead be able to play more
consistently since they'd have a permanent light source.  

  A permanent light source means they always see what's around them:
terrain, monsters, items, etc.  That means that early game experience
with mostly lighted rooms/levels could be used throughout the game.
This is all the more valuable for being used unconsciously.  

  One of my main goals in the game is to reduce the amount of thinking I
have to do.  Yes, many players here are exceptions to that.  That's why
they have trouble giving advice to mere mortals.  They've forgotten what
it's like to be a mere mortal.  They know that what you don't do is just
as important as what you do.  But they've forgotten all those "don'ts"
because they never even think about them any more.  They're hard-coded
into their reflexes instead.  (Ref yesterday's post that watching
ttyrecs of stupid deaths would be better than watching mrivan's
ttyrecs.)

  Carrying the magic lamp means they're more likely to check its buc
status and have a chance to bless it.  And it will still be available
for an emergency wish.



